{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 8, :comments [{:quotes [{:he "טמאים הם לכם", :translit "teme'im hem lachem", :gloss "detestable they are to you"} {:he "את זה תאכלו", :translit "et zeh tocheilu", :gloss "these you may eat"} {:he "חמרא דימא", :translit "chamra d'iyama", :gloss "wine of the sea"}], :mechanisms [{:kind :other, :note "semikhut of adjacent verses cited as hermeneutic basis"} {:kind :other, :note "identification of 'wine of the sea' with an aquatic substance, likely fish-hash (garum/liquamen)"}], :frame {:text "The term 'wine of the sea' (chamra d'iyama) in Aramaic refers to a fermented fish sauce produced from salted and aged fish. Since fish need no ritual slaughter, their byproducts are permitted like the fish themselves (Mishnah Chullin 8:1). The adjacency of 'they are detestable to you' immediately followed by 'these you may eat' (Leviticus 11:9) serves as the textual basis to indicate that an item derived from the sea is permitted. The idea that fish are not given individual names in Scripture because they dwell concealed in the depths reflects a tradition that creatures hidden from human view remain unnamed, as seen in Psalms 104:25 which speaks of innumerable small and great beasts in the sea, and aligns with the rabbinic observation that Scripture enumerates only the land animals at creation (Genesis 2:20) while leaving sea creatures unnamed.", :sources ["Mishnah Chullin 8:1" "Leviticus 11:9" "Psalms 104:25" "Genesis 2:20"]}}], :arithmetic [{:kind :other, :note "semikhut of adjacent verses cited as hermeneutic basis", :by :selah, :ran? false} {:kind :other, :note "identification of 'wine of the sea' with an aquatic substance, likely fish-hash (garum/liquamen)", :by :selah, :ran? false}], :book :leviticus, :voice :baal-haturim, :lang :en, :chapter 11, :discipline "bht-chalkboard-v1", :timestamp "2026-07-12T23:22:49.906115085Z", :model "glm-5.2"}